  "summary": "Three new preprints tie circuit architecture and dynamical regime to locomotion simulation, hippocampal replay dynamics, and structural connectivity recovery.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that circuit behavior depends on architectural constraints and operating regime. The Rat v2 preprint pairs a biomechanical rat body with Rat NCAP, a spinal-circuit-based neural architecture, and GPU training infrastructure to simulate locomotion, placing architectural priors at the center of locomotor control. The in silico study of spatially constrained modular spiking networks shows that spatial constraints and dynamical regime alter how well structural connectivity can be recovered from simulated activity, reversing the direction of inference. The noisy RNN preprint proposes a second-order Langevin sampling framework for hippocampal replay, in which adaptation drives exploration and momentum drives temporal compression. What is new is the explicit link between architectural priors and locomotor control, and between regime dependence and structural inference. The items do not share a common method or system, so the connection rests on the architecture-dynamics relationship rather than on shared tooling.",
